### Definition of the java.lang.Iterable Interface

The **`java.lang.Iterable<T>`** interface is the root superinterface for the entire Java Collection Framework (`Collection<E> extends Iterable<E>`). Implementing this interface allows an object to be the target of the **enhanced for-loop** statement (also known as the `for-each` loop):
```java
package java.lang;
import java.util.Iterator;

public interface Iterable<T> {
    Iterator<T> iterator();
}
```

---

### Compiler De-Sugaring of the Enhanced for-each Loop

The enhanced for-each loop introduced in Java 5 is pure **syntactic sugar**. At compile-time, `javac` translates your high-level for-each loop into an explicit `while` loop utilizing `Iterator`:

```java
// What you write:
for (String item : collection) {
    System.out.println(item);
}

// What the compiler generates in bytecode:
for (Iterator<String> it = collection.iterator(); it.hasNext(); ) {
    String item = it.next();
    System.out.println(item);
}
```
Because the underlying `Iterator` reference is synthetic and inaccessible in code, you **cannot call `iterator.remove()`** inside an enhanced for-each loop. Attempting to call `collection.remove()` directly inside the loop triggers a runtime `ConcurrentModificationException`.

---

### Implementing Custom Iterable on Domain Classes

To make a custom class iterable, implement `Iterable<T>` and return an `Iterator<T>` that implements `hasNext()` and `next()`:
```java
public class Inventory implements Iterable<String> {
    private final String[] items = {"Laptop", "Monitor", "Keyboard"};

    @Override
    public Iterator<String> iterator() {
        return new Iterator<>() {
            private int cursor = 0;
            @Override
            public boolean hasNext() { return cursor < items.length; }
            @Override
            public String next() { return items[cursor++]; }
        };
    }
}
```
Now `for (String item : inventory)` compiles and executes natively.

